---
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Card from '../components/Card.astro';
import Question from '../components/Question.astro';
import Banner from '../components/Banner.astro';
const allWeapons = await getCollection("weapons");
const pageTitle = "Angelic Tools & Tooling";
---
<Layout pageTitle={pageTitle}>
    <Banner
        name="rack"
        alt="A rack of servers, standing on a cloud"
    />
    <h1>Angelic Tools &amp; Tooling</h1>

    <div class="prose">

    <p>
        Off-the-shelf software is very good at the things everybody needs.
        It is the gaps between products that need closing, and that is
        where the work is.
    </p>

    </div>

    <ul role="list" class="link-card-grid">
        {allWeapons.map((card) => <Card
            url={`/weapons/${card.id}/`}
            image={card.data.image.url}
            title={card.data.title}
            body={card.data.description}
            draft={card.data.draft}
        />)}
    </ul>

    <h2>The tools</h2>

    <div class="prose">

    <p>
        A server farm is capacity you can depend on. A server appliance is
        a problem already solved. Bespoke tooling is the integration
        nobody enjoys.
    </p>

    </div>

    <Question
        number={1}
        question="Is the product list right?"
        hint="Three appear in the current copy: server farms, server appliances, bespoke tooling. The persona says do not expose internal module names unless the customer would name them — so AngelBox and Formae are deliberately absent for now. Is that the right call for this page, or does Formae earn its place because customers already use it?"
    />

    <Question
        number={2}
        question="What is each one, in one sentence?"
        hint="A server farm, a server appliance and bespoke tooling each need a sentence that a customer could repeat to their own team. The point of the page is not the list — it is that each product exists to remove a specific kind of pain."
    />

    <Question
        number={3}
        question="Who buys which?"
        hint="A team with a cloud bill and repatriation arithmetic buys a server farm. A team drowning in one operational problem buys an appliance. A team with a bespoke integration buys tooling. Is that the real split, and are there buyers who are not in it?"
    />

    <Question
        number={4}
        question="What does a product cost, and what does it replace?"
        hint="The persona sells outcomes, not features. The repatriation argument is that the savings buy two Oxide rack-scale computers. Is there a comparable number for a server farm and for an appliance — what does the product save, and what does it cost?"
    />

    <Question
        number={5}
        question="What makes one of these a product rather than an assignment?"
        hint="The distinction matters because the assignments page is about services. What does a product include that an assignment does not — a spec, a support commitment, a version, a price list? If the answer is that there is no clean line, say so."
    />

    <Question
        number={6}
        question="What is in the pipeline?"
        hint="The tools that exist, the ones being built, and the ones deliberately not built. Naming the roadmap invites the wrong buyers; not naming it makes the page feel thin. What is the honest state of each product today?"
    />

    <Question
        number={7}
        question="Where does Formae sit?"
        hint="Formae is an infrastructure-as-code platform with its own MCP server and skills, and AngelBox is the framework the estate runs on. If a customer already uses Formae, saying so here earns trust. If not, the names are noise. Decide."
    />

    <Question
        number={8}
        question="The call to action."
        hint="One URL. On a products page it should be the route to buy or to talk about buying — not a link to the documentation, which is where the persona says the builder voice sends people instead."
    />

</Layout>
